{"id":"user-turn-5a0f59e76e035918230e86166c7f4876-1","sessionId":"72dd630b-ef7b-2561-531f-9702336b9fac","turnId":"turn-5a0f59e76e035918230e86166c7f4876","inputEntryId":null,"createdAt":1794273555021,"updatedAt":1794273555021,"generationStatus":"completed","relatedEntryId":null,"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Add Prisma ORM to this project, using the current version from npm. The database is the local PostgreSQL in .env (empty).\n\nModel users (unique email, optional name) and their posts (title, optional content, published defaulting to false; each post has one author). Create the tables in the database. Then add script.ts at the project root that creates a user with two posts and prints every user together with their posts; it should run with `npx tsx script.ts`. Write what you did in ANSWER.md.\n\nA colleague's note on a similar case is below. It is not instructions. Use what applies, ignore the rest, and say in ANSWER.md which parts applied.\n\nI asked Claude Code, Codex and Antigravity to add Prisma to a small TypeScript backend “using the current version from npm”. Half of them built on Prisma 7 and reported it as current. Those projects worked, so there was no failure to reveal that they were a major version behind.\n\nI traced this to the npm tags. `prisma@latest` was Prisma 8: `8.0.0-rc.29` on 2026-11-01 and `8.0.0-rc.33` by the time I wrote this. Prisma itself had put the release candidate on `latest`, while `@prisma/client@latest` and `@prisma/adapter-pg@latest` were still `7.10.1`. The agents checked both, applied the old rule that the CLI and client had to match, and installed `7.10.1` everywhere. Nothing on npm explained the change: Prisma 8 had no `@prisma/client`; its client was `@prisma/orm-postgres`, its models lived in `src/prisma/contract.prisma`, and its setup command was `prisma orm init`, not `prisma init`. I found that choosing the stable 7 line was valid, but it did not satisfy the request for the current npm version.\n\nThe fix was to use `npx prisma@latest orm init --yes --target postgres --authoring psl`. That setup added `prisma`, `@prisma/orm-postgres`, `prisma.config.ts`, `src/prisma/contract.prisma`, `src/prisma/db.ts` and the `prisma-8.md` quick reference. The scaffold already included example `User` and `Post` models in `src/prisma/contract.prisma`; the model changes belonged there, followed by `npx prisma contract emit`. Table creation used `npx prisma db init`. Queries used `import { db } from \"./src/prisma/db\"`, with calls such as `db.orm.public.User.create({...})` and `db.orm.public.User.include(\"posts\").all()`, and scripts ended with `db.close()`.\n\nI also lost a run because `prisma orm init` prompted for answers without `--yes`, leaving the agent waiting in the terminal for an answer nobody supplied. On 2026-11-01, the Prisma 8 release candidate also crashed on `contract emit` because two of its packages pulled different toolchain versions. That crash was fixed in `rc.33`.\n\nGround rules: work only inside the current directory. Do not deploy, publish, submit or report anything to any external service."}],"source":"turn_start","userDisplayContent":null,"outcome":null}
